9829	/// Whether a declared anchor line contains anything a mutation can change
9830	/// (M17.5 F-33 / blind pass 1 A04, A06, A10).
9831	///
9832	/// Operator rules already existed, but only over a mutation's before/after text
9833	/// — i.e. only at stage 1b, where evidence exists. At 1a the packet's declared
9834	/// anchors were checked for POSITION and never for whether the line held any
9835	/// behaviour. Blind pass 1 walked in and found operators anchored to function
9836	/// signatures and enum variants.
9837	///
9838	/// This rejects exactly one thing: a line that DECLARES rather than does. A
9839	/// signature has no predicate to invert, no threshold to shift, no crash point
9840	/// to disable, and no ordering to perturb; whatever the operator, the mutation
9841	/// cannot be applied there, and a mutant that cannot be applied can never be
9842	/// killed while still inflating the denominator §3 counts.
9843	///
9844	/// It deliberately does NOT try to match operators to line contents. A first
9845	/// version did, and rejected three legitimate anchors: `NodeFlags(self.0 |
9846	/// other.0)` is a real inversion site, `if rev == inner.state.head` is a real
9847	/// staleness comparison, and `for entry in entries.flatten()` is a real
9848	/// ordering site. Guessing which token an operator needs produces false
9849	/// accusations against a mutation plan; "is this a declaration" does not.
9850	/// Whether a mutant is GOOD is the 1b runner's question.
9851	/// The aliases a `use` line introduces for the symbols it imports:
9852	/// `use std::fs::{rename as mv, remove_file};` yields `("rename", "mv")`.
9853	/// Blind-review follow-up (review of c455cdf): splitting the whole line on
9854	/// `" as "` mangled a brace group into an alias like `mv, remove_file}`, which
9855	/// matched nothing and reopened the evasion the alias scan closed.
9856	/// `line` with a leading item visibility removed: `pub`, `pub(crate)`,
9857	/// `pub(super)`, `pub(in crate::x)`. Unchanged when it carries none, and
9858	/// unchanged for an identifier that merely starts with those letters.
9859	fn without_visibility(line: &str) -> &str {
9860	    let trimmed = line.trim();
9861	    let Some(rest) = trimmed.strip_prefix("pub") else {
9862	        return trimmed;
9863	    };
9864	    let rest = if let Some(open) = rest.strip_prefix('(') {
9865	        match open.find(')') {
9866	            Some(close) => &open[close + 1..],
9867	            None => return trimmed,
9868	        }
9869	    } else {
9870	        rest
9871	    };
9872	    if rest.starts_with(char::is_whitespace) {
9873	        rest.trim_start()
9874	    } else {
9875	        trimmed
9876	    }
9877	}
